data.content:
Given your single-file library, the **lightest option is `TimeProvider`** (built into .NET 8+). No interfaces to define, no DI container needed—just accept it as a constructor parameter with a default:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
        => _time = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**In tests**, use the built-in fake:

```csharp
var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fakeTime);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

`FakeTimeProvider` lives in `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` (a single NuGet package for your test project only).

**Why this is lightest:**
- No custom interface or wrapper class
- Production callers just do `new InvoiceCalculator()` — no change
- `TimeProvider` is in the BCL (`System` namespace), zero new dependencies for your library

